## 5. Standard Operating Procedures, Guardrails & Escalation Policies

### 5.1 Model Review Gates

| Gate | Criteria | Approver |
|------|----------|----------|
| **Pre-training gate** | Data quality validated; feature coverage acceptable; compute budget approved; training config reviewed | MLE self-check + peer |
| **Post-training gate** | Offline metrics meet threshold; no regression vs. baseline; model card populated | MLE + tech lead |
| **Pre-flight gate** | Offline eval passes; regression gate cleared; launch review doc drafted | Tech lead + PM |
| **Launch gate** | A/B results statistically significant; guardrail metrics green; capacity confirmed | Launch committee (human decision) |
| **Promotion gate** | Steady-state monitoring clean for N days; no latency regression; no drift detected | Service owner + MLE |

### 5.2 Data Quality Checks Before Training

1. **Schema validation** — verify all expected features present with correct types
2. **Null/missing threshold** — fail pipeline if any feature exceeds defined null tolerance (e.g., >5%)
3. **Distribution checks** — compare current data distribution to reference; flag statistical divergence
4. **Freshness validation** — confirm data is within refresh policy window (e.g., 30-day max age)
5. **Deduplication** — verify near-dedup has been applied (MinHash/SimHash)
6. **Tenant opt-out filtering** — re-apply opt-out lists on every training run
7. **Training/serving skew** — validate feature transformations match serving-time behavior

### 5.3 Responsible AI Guardrails

Per [Responsible AI & Ethical Considerations](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/aides/pages/5595534447/Responsible+AI+Ethical+Considerations) and [AI fairness & data risks](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121eeecde7086e94a778d189b8b5d0a90f2/pages/5749449742/AI+fairness+data+risks):

| Guardrail | Implementation |
|-----------|----------------|
| **Bias audits** | Metrics sliced by demographic group; precision/recall parity checks; report false-positive/negative rates per group |
| **Fairness constraints** | Choose and document fairness goal; attempt mitigation (re-weighting, thresholding); record results |
| **Content filtering** | Integrate with platform content filtering and moderation (rai-gptoss sidecar for responsible AI) |
| **Data privacy** | Comply with customer data use terms; zero-data-retention for specified customer cohorts; model training restrictions |
| **Responsible Tech Review** | Complete [No BS Guide to Responsible Technology Reviews](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/aides/pages/5595534447) before launch |
| **Model card + rollback plan** | Must be in place before production deployment |
| **Access controls + audit logs** | All model inference requests logged; access controls enforced |

### 5.4 Rollback Procedures for Model Degradation

| Trigger | Threshold | Action |
|---------|-----------|--------|
| **Latency SLO breach** | P95 latency exceeds KR target for >5 minutes | Auto-alert; human-approved rollback to previous model version |
| **Accuracy degradation** | Online metrics drop >X% vs. baseline (configurable per model) | Auto-alert; pause ramp; investigate root cause |
| **Feature drift** | Distribution divergence exceeds statistical threshold | Auto-alert; trigger data refresh; hold on retraining |
| **Serving errors** | Error rate >1% for >2 minutes | Auto-alert; circuit-break to fallback model |
| **Guardrail violation** | Any guardrail metric breaches safety threshold during A/B | Auto-pause experiment; escalate to launch committee |

**Rollback execution:**
1. Revert TEAMServe endpoint to previous model version
2. Confirm rollback via health check
3. Notify downstream consumers
4. File incident ticket; begin root-cause analysis
5. Post-incident: update model card with failure mode documentation

### 5.5 Escalation Thresholds for Drift Detection

| Level | Signal | Response |
|-------|--------|----------|
| **L1— Informational** | Minor feature drift detected; within expected variance | Log to monitoring dashboard; no action required |
| **L2 — Warning** | Feature drift exceeds 1σ threshold OR model age approaches refresh policy boundary | Alert MLE; schedule investigation; consider early retraining |
| **L3 — Critical** | Concept drift confirmed; online metrics degrading; training/serving skew detected | Page on-call MLE; halt ramp; initiate emergency retraining or rollback |

### 5.6 Approval Workflows for Production Model Updates

Per [AI-First Review Model](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020c681a7783f834e489255dfc7f29e5b4c/pages/6777671071/AI-First+Review+Model+and+SOC+2+PRGB+Compliance):

| Change Type | Risk Tier | Approval Required |
|-------------|-----------|-------------------|
| **Scheduled retraining (same architecture)** | Standard | Automated CI gates + L2 MLE sign-off |
| **Architecture change** | High | Human peer review (≥1 approver); tech lead approval |
| **New model deployment to production** | High/Critical | Launch committee; capacity review; responsible AI review |
| **Emergency rollback** | Critical | On-call MLE can execute immediately; post-incident review required |
| **GPU budget expansion** | High | Platform team + budget owner approval |
| **Cross-team data agreement** | Critical | Data governance + legal review |
| **Secrets/credential changes** | Critical | Human-only; secrets must be handled by human (hard ceiling for autonomous agents) |

### 5.7 Hard Ceilings (Cannot Be Automated Without Policy Changes)

Per the [MLE Agent Capability Evaluation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120202437cfa6229b4f95a58ffcf2c483eb04/pages/6983354773/MLE+Agent+Capability+Evaluation+2026-05-06):

1. **Secrets management** — ASAP keys, Databricks PATs, SLAUTH tokens require human handling
2. **SSAM web UI** — not scriptable via CLI; needs API access or permanent service-account workaround
3. **Judgment calls** — model promotion approval, GPU budget sign-off, "is this model good enough?" need mechanically-checkable rubrics OR accepted human-in-loop gates
4. **Production deploy approvals** — `atlas micros deploy` gates require human approval; granting autonomous deploy rights is a security policy decision

---

## Key Insights for Digital MLE Agent Design

The current MLE Agent capability evaluation shows a clear **front-half/back-half asymmetry**:
- **Front half** (register → load test → eval):85-90% coverage, L2 agency✅
- **Back half** (deploy → monitor → promote → rollback): 10% coverage, L1 agency ❌

The digital MLE handles the **instrumentation and reporting work** that consumes 50-60% of a senior MLE's week — freeing human MLEs to operate at the IC6/IC7 layer of model design, research, and architectural strategy. The three-tier autonomy model (🟢 Autonomous /🟡 Confirm / 🔴 Human) maps directly to the [AI Employee Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6742955831/AI+Employee+Strategy) hot-loop oversight framework.

