## 3. Common MLE Challenges and Failure Modes

### 3.1 Training-Serving Skew

**Definition:** When the model encounters different conditions in production than during training.

**Atlassian-specific manifestation:** "If the Flink job processing Jira webhooks is delayed, your Online Store is stale. However, your Offline Store eventually becomes 'perfect' once the data arrives. This makes your training set'smarter' than production, causing Training-Serving Skew."

**Variants:**
- **Pipeline lag skew:** Feature freshness differences between training data and serving data
- **Feature transformation skew:** Different code paths for training vs. serving feature computation
- **Historical skew:** Retroactive bulk updates (e.g., admin bulk-updating10,000 tickets from 2024) change historical data

**Source:** [Point-in-Time Correctness and Time Management in Feature Store](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6453909241/Point-in-Time+Correctness+and+Time+Management+in+Feature+Store+for+ML+Models)

### 3.2 Data Distribution Drift

**Definition:** When production data distributions diverge from training data over time.

**Categories (per Atlassian documentation):**
1. **Data drift (input distribution changes):** Feature values in production shift vs. what you trained on. Example: "After a platform change or a new migration path, tenants may start having different project sizes/config patterns than historical migrators."
2. **Concept drift (rules of the world change):** The mapping from features → outcome changes. Example: "A cloud optimization reduces the impact of 'number of child issues' on latency. The old model will over-predict risk for large tenants."
3. **Product and ecosystem changes:** New features, configs, limits, or changes in how metrics are computed invalidate learned patterns.
4. **Coverage gaps:** Early models underperform on new segments (very large enterprises, specific industries, unusual configurations).

**Statistical detection methods:**
- Population Stability Index (PSI)
- Kolmogorov-Smirnov test
- Kullback-Leibler divergence
- Mean absolute difference in bin frequencies

**Sources:** [Model drift identification and mitigation strategies](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CA3/pages/1953707277/Model+drift+identification+and+mitigation+strategies), [Crash Course to Machine Learning basics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CT5/pages/6405083048/Crash+Course+to+Machine+Learning+basics)

### 3.3 Reproducibility Failures

**Manifestations:**
- Training runs producing different results due to unseeded randomness
- Environment inconsistencies between development and production
- Dependency version conflicts across ML environments
- Lack of model lineage tracking (what data, what hyperparameters, what code version)

**Atlassian response:** ML Registry, Dreamcatcher (lineage), and Prompt Registry — "covering model versioning, metadata, lineage tracking, and prompt management" to ensure cohesive ownership of model lifecycle management.

**Source:** [(Re-)Introducing Squads in the AU ML Platform Team](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020c9822f67244f4346b0c1bff98957aa5d/blog/2025/11/04/6042990593/Re-+Introducing+Squads+in+the+AU+ML+Platform+Team)

### 3.4 Silent Model Degradation

**Definition:** Model quality degrades without triggering explicit alerts.

**Manifestations:**
- Gradual accuracy decline not caught by threshold-based alerts
- Business metrics diverge from model metrics without connection
- Quality regression visible only in specific segments/populations

**Real example from Atlassian:** "Quality regression in production has a corresponding drift signal (or its absence) on the same observability surface, shortening root-cause analysis." The ML Observability team explicitly built feature drift detection to close this gap: "When quality degrades, we see the symptom but not the cause."

**Source:** [ML Observability - Feature Drift Migration to ML Lens](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-124965)

### 3.5 Feature Store Staleness

**Definition:** Feature values served to models are out of date relative to reality.

**Key gap identified at Atlassian (MLFS SLO Analysis):**
- "The existing SignalFX alert covers only one narrow failure mode (latency budget timeout)"
- "A5xx spike on the read API, a DynamoDB write failure, or a growing SQS backlog would likely not trigger any alert at all"
- "No SignalFX detectors exist that alert on the actual SLO metrics"

**Impact:** "Drift computed on stale data undermines the purpose of drift detection."

**Source:** [MLFS SLO Analysis & Alerting Gaps — May 2026](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020c9822f67244f4346b0c1bff98957aa5d/pages/7020088149/MLFS+SLO+Analysis+Alerting+Gaps+May+2026)

### 3.6 GPU Resource Contention & Infrastructure Challenges

**Manifestations:**
- Shared GPU clusters creating scheduling conflicts
- Inference latency degradation under load (GPU utilization >90% = saturated)
- Scaling too slow during traffic spikes (5-6min provisioning window)
- Instance flapping when scale-in is too aggressive

**Optimization findings:** "Strong evidences that most, if not all, ML models are exhibiting unnecessary added latencies and over-provisioning due to misconfiguration, code inefficiencies and insufficient optimization. Experiments indicate the potential to reduce latencies by 20~30% for models across the entire company."

**Sources:** [ML Latency Optimization Findings](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020ff70b14e5dca488b8ea018f735980b62/pages/4087104885/ML+Latency+Optimization+Findings), [Load testing plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120204d1bd21d680642e6babf8ea1a349b8e3/pages/6788517972/Load+testing+plan)

### 3.7 Overfitting to Offline Metrics

**Classic pattern:** "Validation loss plateaus/worsens in the mid-scale zone (7.5k–17.5k) → suggests dataset-driven issues, not training bugs."

**Atlassian observations:**
- Models showing strong offline gains that don't translate to production A/B improvements
- "Root cause likely lies in dataset quality: noisier labels, negative sampling errors, or distribution shift as scale increases"
- Continual learning attempts causing catastrophic forgetting ("High regression on the base test set")

**Source:** [Gdrive Multi-Task Learning Debug](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120202e103fd9f3ee41278572a7ca1be687e8/pages/5761022932/Gdrive+Multi-Task+Learning+Debug+-+Aug.+28), [Failed Sessions - Goals and Experiment Logs](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/rdm/pages/7002824382/Failed+Sessions+-+Goals+and+Experiment+Logs)

### 3.8 Pipeline Brittleness & Dependency Hell

**Real incident example (HOT-122668):** "All ML Fabric Jobs are Failing" — pipeline failures caused by a databricks init script error. Impact: blocked all ML pipelines across the platform. Resolution required manual cleanup of cloudwatch scripts.

**Common causes:**
- Upstream data schema changes breaking pipelines
- Init script/environment configuration errors
- Library version conflicts
- Single points of failure in shared infrastructure

**Source:** [PIR-28885 - HOT-122668](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/inckb/pages/6056125229/PIR-28885+-+HOT-122668+-+Sev3+-+All+ML+Fabric+Jobs+are+Failing+HOT-122977+-+Sev3+-+Custom+Clusters+are+Failing+in+ML+Studio)

