## 2. Cross-Functional Collaboration Patterns

### 2.1 MLE ↔ Data Scientists (Research Handoff)

**Pattern:** Research-to-production pipeline with structured handoff protocols.

| **Stage** | **DS Responsibility** | **MLE Responsibility** | **Handoff Artifact** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Problem Definition | Strategic analysis, metric definition, feasibility study | Technical feasibility assessment, latency/cost constraints | Problem statement + success metric definition |
| Prototyping | Model architecture exploration, offline experiments | Production viability feedback, infrastructure planning | Notebook with model artifacts + eval results |
| Productionization | Validation support, quality threshold definition | End-to-end pipeline build, serving infrastructure | Production model + monitoring dashboard |
| Iteration | Post-launch analysis, A/B interpretation | Feature engineering, retraining automation | Continuous improvement loop |

**Key insight from Atlassian practice:** "ML Scientist and ML Engineer can be one role" in some team structures. The boundary depends on organizational scale. At startups, this fusion is common.

**Sources:** [How we should organize our team](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7012137e0adfd42d641088c3c5f0b7a5abcd4/pages/3718048461/How+we+should+organize+our+team), [Proposed Experiment Collaboration](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~jcalhoun/pages/2798551672/Proposed+Experiment+Collaboration)

### 2.2 MLE ↔ Product Managers (Business→ML Translation)

**Collaboration model:**
- **Joint Planning:** MLEs and PMs meet with business stakeholders to understand strategic objectives and align technical/analytical capabilities with business needs.
- **Metric Alignment:** PM defines business success metrics; MLE translates to ML proxy metrics (precision, recall, latency).
- **Experiment Design:** Engineering + PM + DS collaborate to design experiments; MLE implements and QAs instrumentation.
- **Impact Reporting:** MLE delivers measurable business impact evidence (metric movements, user-facing improvements).

**Key signal from Atlassian's MLE hiring criteria (P50+):** "Call out their business impacts (e.g., metric movement)" and "Manages cross-functional collaborations independently."

**Source:** [MLE P40-P50 Hiring Assessment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TA/pages/3844969547/MLE+P40+-+P50+Hiring+-+Identifying+Assessing+Machine+Learning+Engineers)

### 2.3 MLE ↔ Platform/SRE Engineers (Model Serving Reliability)

**Collaboration model:**
- **ML Platform Partnership:** "ML engineers (MLE) and ML platform teams should collaborate early and closely in defining and building ML development tools." MLEs focus on creating solutions for use cases; platform teams focus on underlying challenges (privacy control, workflow orchestration, infrastructure integration).
- **Deployment Safety:** Platform provides canary analysis, progressive rollout, and automated rollback. MLE defines model-specific health signals.
- **SLO Co-ownership:** MLE defines model quality SLOs; SRE/platform ensures infrastructure SLOs. Both monitor burn rates.
- **On-call Collaboration:** Joint escalation paths — MLE investigates model quality issues; platform investigates infrastructure failures.

**Source:** [ML Studio: One Pager](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3236010671/ML+Studio+One+Pager)

### 2.4 MLE ↔ Data Engineers (Feature Pipeline Dependencies)

**Collaboration model:**
- **Data Contracts:** Formal upstream SLAs, schema evolution policies, versioning, and backfill/change management agreements.
- **Feature Store Co-ownership:** DE builds ingestion pipelines; MLE defines feature specifications and quality requirements.
- **Quality Monitoring:** Joint responsibility for data quality checks — DE owns pipeline reliability; MLE owns feature correctness.
- **Issue:** "Not having access to TensorFlow GPU cluster — A/B Testing capabilities do not exist, scalability on inference pipeline is an issue" highlights common friction points.

**Sources:** [CoreML Team ML Pipelines Quality Management](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6494524509/CoreML+Team+ML+Pipelines+Quality+Management), [Data Engineering and Data Science Roles in Creating Data Pipelines](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DATA/pages/4504226194/Data+Engineering+and+Data+Science+Roles+in+Creating+Data+Pipelines)

### 2.5 MLE ↔ Security Teams (Model Security, Data Privacy)

**Collaboration model:**
- **Privacy Review:** Use cases requiring tenant-level insights undergo privacy review in ML Studio.
- **Model Governance:** Mandatory compliance checks, model cards, and explainability metrics (SHAP/PFI) before deployment (pattern from Uber's "Model Catalog").
- **Guidance from Atlassian:** "Create or update concrete guidelines defining when to consult privacy team for model onboarding and new API integrations."
- **Data Governance:** Feature store access controls, UGC handling policies, use-case validation via service principals.

**Source:** [MLP Retro Apr 2026](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6882381178/MLP+Retro+Apr+2026+Export+Apr+24+2026+07+05)

### 2.6 MLE ↔ Business Stakeholders (Impact Reporting)

**Pattern:** Regular cadence of impact communication:
- Model performance dashboards tied to business metrics
- A/B test results with business metric impact assessment
- Cost-benefit analysis for model improvements
- Strategic alignment between model capabilities and product roadmap

