## 4. Mitigation Strategies for Each Failure Mode

### 4.1 Canary Deployments for ML Models

**Implementation pattern (from Atlassian practice):**
1. Deploy new model version to small subset of traffic (10%)
2. Monitor health signals during progressive rollout (10% → 50% → 100%)
3. Automatic rollback if issues detected
4. Score-based decisions:
   - Score > 95: High confidence → automatically promote to100%
   - Score 75-95: Marginal → pause and seek manual input
   - Score < 75: High confidence of failure → automatically trigger rollback

**ML-specific canary signals:** HTTP error rates (4xx/5xx), model inference latency, prediction quality metrics, SLO burn rates.

**Key gap (for ML specifically):** "No canary/staged rollout for model deployments. All-or-nothing deployment. Model updates deploy to all instances at once. No way to test with subset of traffic. Bad model causes immediate production impact. Rollback is manual and slow."

**Sources:** [Canary Deployment Implementation Guide](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120203751105336c748498809ee74d33626f3/pages/6372694534/Canary+Deployment+Implementation+Guide), [[Abacus] Canary Deployment for Models](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/CSTE-12653)

### 4.2 Shadow Scoring / Shadow Traffic

**Pattern:** Run new model versions in parallel (shadow mode) against production traffic without affecting users. Compare predictions and latency before promoting.

**Atlassian implementation:** The ML-Powered Deployment Anomaly Detection project uses "Shadow canary analysis runs on an initial set of services" — ML models are trained per-service and run in shadow mode before being used for real canary judgments.

**Stages (Latency Validation Framework):**
1. Development profiling (MSP Launchpad)
2. Shadow traffic evaluation
3. Low-traffic A/B test
4. Statistical significance analysis
5. Full rollout decision

**Pass/Warning/Rollback criteria:**
- ✅ PASS: No statistically significant latency regression + business metrics positive
- ⚠️ WARNING: Minor regression but business metrics positive → hold at current percentage
- ❌ ROLLBACK: Significant latency regression, user-impacting issues, or business metrics negative

**Source:** [Latency Validation Framework for Model Deployment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120204aa07fe7450f489899b60900ad6a0967/pages/5981244955/Latency+Validation+Framework+for+Model+Deployment)

### 4.3 Automated Drift Detection

**Implementation at Atlassian (ML Lens platform):**
- **Statistical tests:** KS for numeric features, Chi-squared for categorical — auto-selected per feature type
- **No per-feature configuration required:** Calculator automatically selects applicable test
- **Metric publishing:** Drift results publish as metric series alongside model quality metrics
- **Alert configuration:** Configurable sensitivity levels, multiple stakeholders can set different thresholds on same metric

**Architecture decisions:**
- Drift detection belongs in the observability platform (not training/experimentation)
- Best data freshness via direct metric store integration
- "Drift computed on stale data undermines the purpose of drift detection" — freshness is critical

**Tools used:** Population Stability Index (PSI), KL Divergence, Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, Feature Drift (mean absolute bin frequency difference).

**Sources:** [Drift Detection Metrics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/agd/pages/6192530763/Drift+Detection+Metrics), [ML Observability - Feature Drift Migration to ML Lens](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-124965)

### 4.4 Feature Importance Monitoring & Quality SLOs

**Proactive Quality Management (CoreML approach):**
- Feature store & data quality checks
- Data contracts with upstream teams
- Observability (drift/versioning)
- Pipeline monitoring & alerting
- Downstream SLAs

**Feature quality controls (MLFS):**
- Input validation (data type verification, null rates, schema adherence)
- Metrics collection (row count, null count/%, min/max, mean, stddev, histograms)
- Distribution monitoring & drift detection
- Freshness & consistency tracking
- Alertable signals for degradation

**Target state:** "All in-prod features are centralized in Feature Store, with full quality check" (FY26Q4 milestone).

**Source:** [CoreML Team ML Pipelines Quality Management](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6494524509/CoreML+Team+ML+Pipelines+Quality+Management), [[DRAFT] MLFS Feature Quality Control](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5537533542/DRAFT+MLFS+Feature+Quality+Control+An+Overview)

### 4.5 Fallback Model Strategies

**Pattern:**
- Maintain champion model as instant rollback target
- "Pause if SLO breach (reliability, latency)" during experimentation
- Version-controlled model deployments with one-click rollback
- Pre-launch checklist must be filled before production launch

**Deployment lifecycle:**
1. Model passes initialization health check
2. Model scores are as expected on test set
3. End-to-end latency is within KR
4. Experimentation capacity available
5. Monitor metrics continuously
6. Production launch only after pre-launch checklist

**Source:** [How To: Deploy ML Models to TEAMServe](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120204aa07fe7450f489899b60900ad6a0967/pages/5959589965/How+To+Deploy+ML+Models+to+TEAMServe)

### 4.6 Training-Serving Skew Mitigation

**Specific strategies from Atlassian:**
- **Monitor freshness (Ts - Te):** If lag is high, "lag-offset" offline joins so model trains on the same staleness it encounters in production
- **Watermarking:** Keep data tiles "open" for a grace period; late-arriving data handled as exception
- **Historical skew detection:** Use System Time to detect retroactive bulk updates; flag Historical Skew alerts
- **Consistent feature computation:** Feature store ensures training and serving use identical transformation code

**Source:** [Point-in-Time Correctness and Time Management in Feature Store](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6453909241/Point-in-Time+Correctness+and+Time+Management+in+Feature+Store+for+ML+Models)

### 4.7 MLOps Retraining Strategies

**Scheduled vs. Event-Triggered:**
- **Scheduled:** Retrain every N days. Simple, but risky if drift happens between retrains.
- **Event-Triggered:** Retrain only when accuracy drops or data drift is detected. Complex, but efficient.
- **Hybrid (recommended):** Scheduled retraining with event-triggered overrides.

**MLOps pipeline pattern:**
```
Production → Monitoring → Drift > Threshold? → YES → Extract → Retrain → Evaluate vs Champion → Better? → Deploy
```

**Source:** [Crash Course to Machine Learning basics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CT5/pages/6405083048/Crash+Course+to+Machine+Learning+basics)

