## 5. Team Structure and Organizational Fit (Startup Context)

### 5.1 Organizational Models for ML Teams

Based on industry patterns and Atlassian's own evolution, three primary structures exist:

| **Model** | **Pros** | **Cons** | **Best For** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **By business vertical** (ML embedded in product teams) | Work together within team to deliver business impact | Ownership of shared components unclear | Product-focused startups with distinct verticals |
| **By craft** (separate ML eng and SWE orgs) | Clear structure even as org expands | Separation of skillsets that need to work closely together | Large orgs with many ML use cases |
| **By technical layer** (serving layer vs. ML/infrastructure layer) | Clear structure even as org expands | No single team has end-to-end picture | Platform companies with multiple consumers |

**Key insight for startups:** "Software engineers and ML engineers need to work more closely together" — startups should default to embedded/fused models rather than separate craft organizations.

**Source:** [Yang's perspective to ...](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020cb0062c035e24d1ea09cd052b0f12dad/pages/3680113182/Yang+s+perspective+to+...)

### 5.2 Recommended Startup Team Structure

**For an early-stage startup (2-10 person ML team):**

```
CTO / Head of AI
├── MLE 1 (Senior/Staff) — Full-stack ML, end-to-end model lifecycle
├── MLE 2 — Model development + feature engineering
├── ML Platform Eng — Infrastructure, serving, MLOps
├── Data Engineer (shared/embedded) — Pipeline reliability
└── Data Scientist (optional, can be fused with MLE)
```

**Atlassian's role definition for reference:**
- **PM:** Facilitate discussion, alignment, roadmap
- **Data Scientist:** Work close with domain expert to get biz requirements, analytics, storytelling
- **ML Scientist:** Model prototyping, development, R&D
- **ML Engineer:** Infrastructure, model deployment, MLOps
- **DE:** Data pipeline, dashboard pipeline
- *"ML Scientist and ML Engineer can be one role."*

**Source:** [How we should organize our team](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7012137e0adfd42d641088c3c5f0b7a5abcd4/pages/3718048461/How+we+should+organize+our+team)

### 5.3 Reporting Relationships

**Startup context:**
- MLE reports to **Head of AI/ML** or **CTO** directly in early stage
- As team grows: MLE → Engineering Manager (M3) → Head of AI/Engineering
- At Atlassian scale: "1 Engineering Manager (M3), 2x Machine Learning Engineers (P5), 2x Sr. Software Engineers (Back-end, P5)" is the typical team pod

**Team structure principles (from ML Platform):**
1. Impact and results (measured by OKRs and operational metrics)
2. Team health (measured in Health Monitors)
3. People career growth (measured in Performance Reviews)

**Source:** [Machine Learning Platform - Teams Structure](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/1017005145/Machine+Learning+Platform+-+Teams+Structure)

### 5.4 Expected Autonomy Level

The autonomy model from Atlassian's AI Employee framework provides an excellent framework for human MLEs too:

| **Tier** | **Domain** | **Autonomy Level** |
|---|---|---|
| **Data & Signal Engineering** | Monitoring and alerting | 🟢 Autonomous |
| | New signal ingestion | 🟡 Confirm with lead |
| | Cross-team data agreements | 🔴 Requires human (PM/leadership) |
| **Model Training & Iteration** | Scheduled retraining and comparison | 🟢 Autonomous |
| | Architecture changes | 🟡 Confirm with lead |
| | Model design decisions | 🔴 Requires human (tech lead) |
| **Offline Evaluation** | Eval execution and summary | 🟢 Autonomous |
| | Regression interpretation | 🟡 Confirm with lead |
| | Launch recommendation | 🔴 Requires human (PM + eng lead) |
| **Production Deployment** | Standard deployment with canary | 🟢 Autonomous |
| | Rollback decisions | 🟡 Autonomous with notification |
| | Novel infrastructure changes | 🔴 Requires platform team |

**For startups:** Higher autonomy is expected. A senior MLE in a startup context should own the full model lifecycle with minimal oversight, escalating only on cross-team dependencies and strategic decisions.

**Source:** [Digital MLE: AI Employee Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727/Digital+MLE+AI+Employee+Vision)

### 5.5 Ownership Boundaries

**Full model lifecycle ownership (startup default):**
The MLE owns end-to-end: data pipeline → feature engineering → model training → evaluation → deployment → monitoring → retraining.

**Atlassian MLE role specification:**
- "The Atlassian MLE role spans the full ML lifecycle: signal engineering, model training and evaluation, experiment design, offline/online eval, and production deployment."
- P50+ expectation: "Experience in building large scale production ML models with measurable impact" + "Experience tech leading2-3 projects"

**Source:** [Digital MLE: AI Employee Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727/Digital+MLE+AI+Employee+Vision)

### 5.6 On-Call Responsibilities for ML Systems

**Pattern from Atlassian's MLOps Weekly Handoff:**
- "Detailed guide for on-call machine learning engineers (MLEs) responsible for monitoring, investigating, and resolving issues related to search quality, platform costs, and customer satisfaction metrics"
- Key responsibilities: operations monitoring, issue investigation, resolution, and escalation
- "Ensure operational excellence and continuous improvement"

**On-call model for ML teams:**
1. **Primary responsibility:** Keep ML serving systems healthy — triage and resolve support requests and alerts
2. **Secondary responsibility:** Contributing to tech debt backlog and service improvements
3. **Handoff:** Weekly sync with previous on-call for outstanding follow-ups
4. **Scope:** Model quality alerts, pipeline failures, feature store issues, inference latency degradation

**Escalation path (from Abacus SLOs example):**
1. Team on-call engineer
2. Domain DRI (e.g., Model owner)
3. ML Platform team (infrastructure issues)

**Sources:** [HOWTO: MLOPs Weekly Handoff Meeting](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SRCA/pages/6107906382/HOWTO+MLOPs+Weekly+Handoff+Meeting), [On-call responsibilities](https://developer.atlassian.com/cloud/enterprise-posture-management/tech-ops/on-call)

### 5.7 Squad Structure for Scaling

As the ML team grows beyond 5-6 engineers, Atlassian recommends domain-aligned squads:

**Example (AU ML Platform Team):**
1. **Feature Store Squad** — owns feature ingestion, storage, materialisation, serving
2. **Observability Squad** — owns ML Lens, AI Platform UI, model performance visibility
3. **Registry Squad** — owns model versioning, metadata, lineage, prompt management

**Benefits:**
- End-to-end responsibility per squad
- Quick decision-making within domain
- Reduced context switching
- Shared tooling, operational practices, and on-call responsibilities across squads
- Squad-level OKRs provide structured planning

**Source:** [(Re-)Introducing Squads in the AU ML Platform Team](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020c9822f67244f4346b0c1bff98957aa5d/blog/2025/11/04/6042990593/Re-+Introducing+Squads+in+the+AU+ML+Platform+Team)

